    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Numbers\n\n* **Current Meta Comp (IC6):**\n  * Base: $240,000\n  * Target Bonus (~15%): $36,000\n  * Annual RSU run-rate: ~$250,000\n  * **Total Current Run-Rate:** **~$526,000/yr** ($276k cash + $250k stock)\n\n* **Google Offer (L6):**\n  * Base: $235,000 *($5,000 pay cut on base)*\n  * Target Bonus (20%): $47,000\n  * Equity: $1,080,000 over 4 years (~$270,000/yr average)\n  * Sign-on Bonus: $0\n  * **4-Year Annualized Total Comp:** **~$552,000/yr** ($282k cash + $270k stock)\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**It is a standard initial offer, but it is not compelling enough to leave Meta.**\n\n* **Marginal upside on run-rate:** On a 4-year annualized basis, Google's offer ($552k) is only **~5% higher** than your Meta run-rate ($526k).\n* **Base pay is lower:** They are offering $235k, whereas you make $240k and the posted range tops out at $271k.\n* **No transition protection:** Leaving Meta means walking away from $200k in near-term RSUs over the next 12 months, and Google offered **$0 sign-on bonus**.\n* **Vesting curve considerations:** Google often utilizes front-loaded equity vesting (e.g., 33%/33%/22%/12% or 38%/32%/20%/10%). While this boosts Years 1 and 2, without a sign-on bonus or higher baseline equity, Year 3 and Year 4 drop off steeply unless refresher grants are substantial.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Verdict: Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou hold maximum leverage:\n1. Google reached out to you.\n2. You are happy and performing at Meta.\n3. You have an active high-paying baseline and unvested stock.\n\nDo not accept this initial offer, but do not walk away without negotiating. Google expects a counter-offer at L6.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For (and Exact Targets)\n\nAt Google L6, top-of-band total compensation packages regularly reach **$600,000 \u2013 $650,000+ annualized**, with strong sign-on bonuses.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Matches/beats your current $240k base; sits comfortably below the $271k cap. |\n| **Equity (4-yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,350,000** | ~$312k\u2013$337k/yr average; pushes the equity component into the 75th+ percentile for L6. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets the $200,000 of Meta RSUs you forfeit in the next 12 months. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nCommunicate this over the phone with your recruiter, followed by a brief summary email:\n\n> *\"I really enjoyed the interview process and the team/scope is exciting. However, as I mentioned early on, I\u2019m happy at Meta, performing well, and wasn't actively looking to move.*\n> \n> *My current run-rate at Meta is roughly $526,000, and I have about $200,000 in equity vesting over the next 12 months. Taking a slight reduction on base salary ($235k vs my current $240k) and a 4-year annualized total comp around $552k doesn't provide enough financial justification for the risk of switching.*\n> \n> *To make this an easy 'yes' for me, we'd need to bridge that gap:*\n> 1. *Base salary closer to **$255,000\u2013$260,000**.*\n> 2. *An equity grant around **$1.3M** over 4 years.*\n> 3. *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000\u2013$100,000** to help offset the unvested equity I leave behind at Meta.*\n> \n> *If Google can get close to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\nIf Google refuses to move significantly on equity or sign-on bonus, you are in the ideal position to politely decline and remain at Meta.",
